#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091934 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "48d7b03b-c991-574a-bf15-8765ca43724b" :question/math-academy-id "e-11113" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of $X_1$ and $X_2$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1^2=9,\\qquad \\sigma_2^2 = 1, \\qquad \\rho = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac1k\\,\\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ is the mean vector and $\\Sigma$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2_i = \\textrm{Var}[X_i],$ $\\sigma_{12} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2],$ and $\\rho$ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf X$ is\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf x - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nand $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma.$\n\nUsing the given values of $\\sigma^2_1, \\sigma^2_2$, and $\\rho,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n![](images/f4/f44326b7be98378285978658be508da822c110d5d61e8950872cc75c4296d7ae.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\Sigma| = 9\\cdot 1 - 1\\cdot 1 = 8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{8}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi \\sqrt{2}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi\\sqrt 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "90ff4bf9-f8d3-5179-9d02-0e0a46a3cc98" :question/math-academy-id "e-11114" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1,X_2]^T$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = 3X_1 + X_2 .$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\mu_1 = 2$ and $\\mu_2 = -1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+(-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have $\\sigma_1^2 = 4, \\sigma_2^2 = 7,$ and $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = -3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[3{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 4+{1}^{2}\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =36+7-18 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(5,25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2025f8-a9cf-5e30-bb03-4909aefdd7e2" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Multivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "f9e6c722-6017-57b9-9de6-c83c2f14c8c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11115" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2,X_3]^T$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y = X_1 -2X_2 +4 X_3,$ we have \n\n![](images/12/129ff41cb0fc5917efdc4f990d3d2efeb7546911eadb52340156f1ddc4f9d92f.svg)\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1-4+4 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =12-6+56 \\\\  & =62.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY\\sim N(1,62).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09914385-0a05-5f0a-babc-48e716b5d3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{15})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548372} {:answer/id #uuid "22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548421} {:answer/id #uuid "2d44a4ab-bf70-577b-95d4-802bedc72e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,17)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548444} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5b2179-34b2-5f28-a785-1f671241e094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{17})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548660} {:answer/id #uuid "dc53f2d3-d6bf-569d-bb68-e47e1dbbc0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,\\sqrt{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22382cd6-21f0-5384-9d9e-e555a766fac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(-2,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548421} :answer-field/id #uuid "e35154dd-0662-5424-962f-17ec732aca8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "695d4d94-877b-5df0-9f8f-05a7c0cd7954" :question/math-academy-id "q-143542" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4c0cb8-d9a4-5c3a-9a2d-8f3bcc6f2d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548384} {:answer/id #uuid "59b192f8-8a08-5e40-93d5-ab8e3f2938f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548520} {:answer/id #uuid "843df82d-bd20-5e2b-8054-24ab30500319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548609} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0c62a2-2941-508f-8b34-49fd4599dbf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548726} {:answer/id #uuid "fca68fd5-18c4-580f-8af1-5f191fa77ca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59b192f8-8a08-5e40-93d5-ab8e3f2938f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548520} :answer-field/id #uuid "e306f3ba-57fb-5958-a190-c31ffdd43476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0707e3bd-3da9-5662-809b-216f2be6b97a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159689" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their covariance are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=10,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{12}=5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and ${\\sigma }_{12},$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=10\\cdot 5-5\\cdot 5=25.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{25}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=10\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a17a8449-31bb-525a-8ca5-1e66b4a439fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1711" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089652} {:answer/id #uuid "a49337cb-0f46-5c90-84d8-7f669ba7f29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1762" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089653} {:answer/id #uuid "d019c2f7-49d1-571b-ac68-b7db432075f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1977" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089654} {:answer/id #uuid "de43eab0-c4d8-5a9d-b725-ecc7339189c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2033" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089655} {:answer/id #uuid "4eaee5a4-a5b3-5342-a3a6-6a78faf919f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de43eab0-c4d8-5a9d-b725-ecc7339189c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2033" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089655} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5efdcd4-17d6-5391-871c-f2a692148bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89c577b9-8c2b-5e60-9161-7d08d52dcc46" :question/math-academy-id "q-159694" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(Y\\ge 1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]-2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}-2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+16-4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(-2,13).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\ge 1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-(-2)}{\\sqrt{13}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.832)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-P(Z<0.83) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.83) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.83)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.83)=0.7967.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.83) & =1-Φ(0.83) \\\\  & =1-0.7967 \\\\  & =0.2033.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=0.2033.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2025f8-a9cf-5e30-bb03-4909aefdd7e2" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Multivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a20292c9-1982-585c-be36-9420091bb57e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089658} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e8eb56-0371-5757-8469-9c0697832e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2810" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089659} {:answer/id #uuid "b73494d1-fc66-51a2-9f26-71a8c9140462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089660} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1516df-74a2-5d5b-89ee-857bc7a12a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3062" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089661} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0330de-9644-59fe-8b6a-f5a8dcc656bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3783" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5e8eb56-0371-5757-8469-9c0697832e0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2810" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089659} :answer-field/id #uuid "63118e31-c8a5-57cd-b21b-61395ca13c09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "719754e3-0e30-5697-91d7-a36d391d48f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159695" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$ Find $P(Y<1).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ | $\\,0.7190\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ | $\\,0.7517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ | $\\,0.7823\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ | $\\,0.8078\\,$ | $\\,0.8106\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+2-2 \\\\  & =3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3+10-1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(3,12).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y<1).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<1) & =P(Z<\\frac{1-3}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-0.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58) & =P(Z\\le -0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.58)=0.7190.\n$$\n\nBy the symmetry of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.58)=1-Φ(0.58)=1-0.7190=0.2810.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.58)=0.2810.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y<1)=0.2810.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0e1aba-d9b8-59e9-9c12-caf928724ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548374} {:answer/id #uuid "638f0ce8-9e40-5e97-b676-b714876cc977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548544} {:answer/id #uuid "ca644bff-bc2d-5268-9f93-15a088f842f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548753} {:answer/id #uuid "df425403-018d-5628-b418-23b7c205731c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548787} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f879ab-0152-5dc2-bd80-fa45cb37146f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a0e1aba-d9b8-59e9-9c12-caf928724ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548374} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b850ea-8926-5c8f-8acd-249aeec872ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7dfbbbd-1a36-5131-be23-c3a22a696cb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159737" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their covariance are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=10,\\,{\\sigma }_{12}=-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and ${\\sigma }_{12},$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=2\\cdot 10-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{4}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=4\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63d4b36e-a125-56e6-8dc4-26be28600df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548545} {:answer/id #uuid "929fe2cb-8abf-58de-b2d1-55a8dc7e08de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548641} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a9103e-fc08-541d-9d5c-4caa1baddaf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548670} {:answer/id #uuid "bb9e42d2-b0f5-554b-badc-9745ae7fdb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548727} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3f8cc3d-0caa-56a5-a312-f8f8ce8113b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548840} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b0e0da-9a99-5474-9c0a-b2662fde58bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33c4f786-72e7-5bcb-ae24-ddbd9884bcad" :question/math-academy-id "q-159742" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,\\rho =\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and $\\rho ,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=5\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 4=9.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{9}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=6\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ea11954-6a4f-5d76-99d3-931555d6a5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548413} {:answer/id #uuid "44909d7b-b627-5b06-8717-189b1d4b5122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548484} {:answer/id #uuid "593fcdbb-3bf7-51f3-8353-2a0bb906561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548518} {:answer/id #uuid "cd5b40e6-2599-5535-a953-6e2b8d5c5bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548757} {:answer/id #uuid "e51b59d3-e3d9-56a2-bc23-364e27227cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44909d7b-b627-5b06-8717-189b1d4b5122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548484} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e8b48ff-d943-5ecb-9098-05bbf34d8cc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4cb9239-3b4d-5884-8d60-86ece34323bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159743" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=20,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=2,\\,\\rho =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and $\\rho ,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=20\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=36.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{36}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=12\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246df32d-4746-5885-a035-d11664f353a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548427} {:answer/id #uuid "3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548471} {:answer/id #uuid "84f8a713-083b-567a-87f3-b56ca285c20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548612} {:answer/id #uuid "9385d1b3-a5a1-5507-b599-cbba0c48627c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548643} {:answer/id #uuid "d25bb8b0-15e2-5a36-ad57-03625d178a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dcc97e2-1cf4-58b0-8ccd-6aaf27892d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548471} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e974d9-137d-5422-9c75-5e0f9c76f643" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c05d07c3-d88b-5f15-ae17-7072be0fa8fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-159744" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector. The variances of ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ and their correlation coefficient are given by\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=20,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,\\,\\rho =-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu $ is the mean vector and $Σ$ is the corresponding covariance matrix, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =(\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}),\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\sigma }_{i}^{2}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ ${\\sigma }_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}],$ and $\\rho $ is the correlation, then the joint probability density function of $X$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu ))\n$$\n\nand $|Σ|$ denotes the determinant of $Σ.$\n\nUsing the given values of ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$, and $\\rho ,$ our covariance matrix is\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}20 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|Σ|=20\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot (-6)=64.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into our expression for the joint PDF, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{64}}exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16\\pi }exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(x-\\mu )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $k=16\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbfe970-6da3-5f5a-8d7f-66bdfdaa11d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548414} {:answer/id #uuid "275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(3,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548433} {:answer/id #uuid "483d5766-64b0-58a2-a33c-dd47e2b32667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548493} {:answer/id #uuid "b8326e47-f122-5282-9791-357440f05fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(6,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548722} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b54e42-31fd-5bc1-b149-fc46cf14e05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(6,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "275ad22b-2609-5ea6-b5e7-33bd6dd56aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(3,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548433} :answer-field/id #uuid "31af4987-ee12-5601-b83b-d991adf87071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "485fd77f-8eb8-5250-aed7-606fdfdfa4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159747" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=0$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=2,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2+4+2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(3,8).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67dbfefa-4caa-5758-9ac2-e1c248073a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5987" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548551} {:answer/id #uuid "6f24b879-634d-55bb-9903-039b6caaac8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548563} {:answer/id #uuid "82b2c84e-fc23-5e11-8368-7f6cfde6c81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6443" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548605} {:answer/id #uuid "8c21487c-b2e7-534d-af44-5ceea08e6498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5636" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548626} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbb04af-60be-5e8f-b9e6-9d32e8c07a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6368" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f24b879-634d-55bb-9903-039b6caaac8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548563} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c46b911-92e9-5ee5-b90b-d6b9b56e65d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de58daf1-e39b-5442-89f0-7a159aadbac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159776" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y=2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(Y\\le 10).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+4\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =2{\\mu }_{1}+4{\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =2(-2)+4(3) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=2,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[2{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={2}^{2}(2)+{4}^{2}(4)+2(2)(4)(-1) \\\\  & =8+64-16 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(8,56).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(Y\\le 10).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 10) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{10-8}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.27)=0.6064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 10)=0.6064.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07524969-032c-5c00-ab25-ee4291f1a0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2002" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548369} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfe9eed-26f1-58ae-878b-1a814c7aca9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1762" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548403} {:answer/id #uuid "23f237eb-8701-5484-ae9d-933afafe1c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548426} {:answer/id #uuid "45c0db7b-6f4a-57f4-80c3-8f88590dd601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1558" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548487} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9a8cf30-b566-51f5-a9f4-f21f56e8c936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548813} :answer-field/id #uuid "d466cd72-cb8d-5d38-94b7-6314b0963b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b8afbc1-6271-57eb-9548-1efb5cfd889f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159778" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}.$ Calculate $P(1\\le Y\\le 5).$\n\n*Hint: Tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1151\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1357\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1587\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2643\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2981\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3409\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3372\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3336\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=5$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+3\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={\\mu }_{1}+3\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =5+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=1,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=5,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}] \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 1+{3}^{2}\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+45-12 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nY∼N(8,34).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to compute $P(1\\le Y\\le 5).$ First, transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le Y\\le 5) & =P(\\frac{1-8}{\\sqrt{34}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{5-8}{\\sqrt{34}}) \\\\  & =P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51) & =P(Z\\le -0.51)-P(Z<-1.20) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.51)-P(Z\\le -1.20) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.51)-Φ(-1.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.20) & =0.1151 \\\\ Φ(-0.51) & =0.3050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.20\\le Z\\le -0.51) & =Φ(-0.51)-Φ(-1.20) \\\\  & =0.3050-0.1151 \\\\  & =0.1899.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(1\\le Y\\le 5)=0.1899.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2025f8-a9cf-5e30-bb03-4909aefdd7e2" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Multivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(-3,64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548668} {:answer/id #uuid "a63863ed-8164-57d5-b1c1-927b3010411d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(2,71)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548682} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bcf75e-464c-51d2-bf87-67feb71d6670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(-1,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548712} {:answer/id #uuid "bacaf94a-b2f5-5841-93ff-7fce330f4aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(-3,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548725} {:answer/id #uuid "ef55d0bd-aea2-59fb-9676-cd9c08bfd13f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(2,64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fce381d-1327-590b-a9d0-bbac5c3547d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(-3,64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548668} :answer-field/id #uuid "67479386-cd07-5c4b-bace-808cced8a369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5760ff2c-7d2e-56c5-873e-f2312179cd86" :question/math-academy-id "q-159788" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}-{X}_{2}+3{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2-2-3 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =9-5+60 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-3,64).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2025f8-a9cf-5e30-bb03-4909aefdd7e2" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Multivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(-7,25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548418} {:answer/id #uuid "85d3f3d0-e0a2-51c3-88b5-be41b56f5657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(4,13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548614} {:answer/id #uuid "8783e7e5-5510-5cd7-9832-3afa73d7110a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(-7,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548615} {:answer/id #uuid "96aaff8a-9da2-5003-b65f-22ecdfada8d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(4,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548647} {:answer/id #uuid "e90be331-8dd6-5180-9867-a9f8b8fd88fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(1,25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "212a264a-c734-5158-a429-631f6037efd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(-7,25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548418} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c7e05ff-0edc-5d15-8a6d-ef02a4eb8812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "279fa865-ddd8-5bba-a76f-e827f503b5bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-159790" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=2{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2-1-4 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =14+4+7 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(-7,25).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2025f8-a9cf-5e30-bb03-4909aefdd7e2" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Multivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(13,67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548388} {:answer/id #uuid "21764b22-3a61-5f2b-9d32-d852559b4581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(5,34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548419} {:answer/id #uuid "4d051156-4080-5cde-a8d8-8d8f091756ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(13,51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548502} {:answer/id #uuid "9a11b056-cc10-5a5c-8bc6-2565c9be5efc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(17,51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548659} {:answer/id #uuid "ad020fb2-061b-5feb-8d6a-974e236033c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(5,67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eedeb12-ec11-56ff-b29b-e3751160e5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(13,67)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548388} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8cb4950-ba1f-5989-a857-8e8855ec8b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7717a0a-c395-5f69-ba3c-1ca2b2b29ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159794" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+3{X}_{2}+2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+9+4 \\\\  & =13,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-5+42+30 \\\\  & =67.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(13,67).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2025f8-a9cf-5e30-bb03-4909aefdd7e2" :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Multivariate Normal Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ccae1b9-35e7-5303-9d4c-35ad96bcbc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(6,19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548405} {:answer/id #uuid "407f15e3-cc95-57e6-baf2-1e8c8b85f9a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(4,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548477} {:answer/id #uuid "458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(4,19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548485} {:answer/id #uuid "52c5f538-dabe-5a48-92c1-4333dc4b66bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(2,21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548509} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c79a96-262b-5861-aff5-4efc84fd373d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(6,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "458f1ea7-cfea-59ee-baf1-e528c0816a4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(4,19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548485} :answer-field/id #uuid "53378554-b1a5-553b-8166-b2092af85775" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a69ab04f-b52a-5382-92a0-123e6cfce6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159906" :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]^{T}$ be a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{k}]^{T}$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY={c}^{T}X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{c}_{i}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable, where $c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, since we wish to know the distribution of $Y=-{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3},$ we have \n\n$$\nc=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+3+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the variance of $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6-1+14 \\\\  & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(4,19).\n$$"}]}
